1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,371
What if you could sleep

2
00:00:01,371 --> 00:00:02,520
with half your brain,

3
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:04,409
while the other half stays awake?

4
00:00:04,429 --> 00:00:06,020
Dolphins do it every single day.

5
00:00:07,020 --> 00:00:08,329
One eye open.

6
00:00:08,349 --> 00:00:09,991
Half the brain snoozing.

7
00:00:10,011 --> 00:00:11,471
It's not a party trick,

8
00:00:11,491 --> 00:00:12,726
it's how they survive.

9
00:00:12,746 --> 00:00:13,946
Let's find out why,

10
00:00:13,966 --> 00:00:14,849
and what would happen if

11
00:00:14,849 --> 00:00:15,699
you could do it too.

12
00:00:18,699 --> 00:00:20,389
Your brain has two halves,

13
00:00:20,409 --> 00:00:21,603
called hemispheres.

14
00:00:21,623 --> 00:00:22,543
When you fall asleep,

15
00:00:22,563 --> 00:00:24,657
both halves slow down together.

16
00:00:24,677 --> 00:00:25,830
But a dolphin's brain can

17
00:00:25,830 --> 00:00:26,767
split the shift:

18
00:00:26,787 --> 00:00:28,477
one half drops into deep,

19
00:00:28,497 --> 00:00:29,717
slow-wave sleep,

20
00:00:29,737 --> 00:00:31,362
while the other half stays awake.

21
00:00:31,382 --> 00:00:32,985
Later, they swap.

22
00:00:33,005 --> 00:00:33,983
Scientists call it

23
00:00:33,983 --> 00:00:35,919
unihemispheric slow-wave sleep.

24
00:00:35,939 --> 00:00:37,053
It was first measured in

25
00:00:37,053 --> 00:00:38,399
the 1970s,

26
00:00:38,419 --> 00:00:39,759
by recording the brain waves

27
00:00:39,759 --> 00:00:40,683
of sleeping dolphins.

28
00:00:42,033 --> 00:00:43,646
Here's the clever part.

29
00:00:43,666 --> 00:00:44,656
In dolphins,

30
00:00:44,676 --> 00:00:45,905
nearly all the wiring from

31
00:00:45,905 --> 00:00:47,095
each eye crosses over to

32
00:00:47,095 --> 00:00:48,680
the opposite half of the brain.

33
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:49,639
So when the left half

34
00:00:49,639 --> 00:00:50,600
is asleep,

35
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:51,559
it's often the left eye

36
00:00:51,559 --> 00:00:52,740
that stays open,

37
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:53,787
because it's wired to the

38
00:00:53,787 --> 00:00:54,796
half that's still awake.

39
00:00:55,796 --> 00:00:57,219
So why sleep in halves?

40
00:00:57,239 --> 00:00:58,898
The leading idea is breathing.

41
00:00:58,918 --> 00:01:00,198
You breathe automatically,

42
00:01:00,218 --> 00:01:01,523
even in deep sleep.

43
00:01:01,543 --> 00:01:02,556
Dolphins are voluntary

44
00:01:02,556 --> 00:01:03,782
breathers: each breath is

45
00:01:03,782 --> 00:01:04,533
something they actively do,

46
00:01:04,553 --> 00:01:06,013
at the surface,

47
00:01:06,033 --> 00:01:07,136
through the blowhole on top

48
00:01:07,136 --> 00:01:07,864
of their head.

49
00:01:07,884 --> 00:01:09,094
Keeping half the brain awake

50
00:01:09,094 --> 00:01:10,229
lets them keep coming up

51
00:01:10,229 --> 00:01:12,177
for that next breath.

52
00:01:12,197 --> 00:01:13,346
Half-awake may also help them

53
00:01:13,346 --> 00:01:14,147
watch for predators,

54
00:01:14,167 --> 00:01:15,727
stay with their group,

55
00:01:15,747 --> 00:01:17,231
and keep moving to stay warm.

56
00:01:17,251 --> 00:01:18,662
Researchers still debate how

57
00:01:18,662 --> 00:01:20,205
much each reason matters.

58
00:01:20,225 --> 00:01:21,349
Some even reported that

59
00:01:21,349 --> 00:01:22,511
newborn dolphin calves and

60
00:01:22,511 --> 00:01:23,617
their mothers barely rest at

61
00:01:23,617 --> 00:01:25,455
all for weeks after birth,

62
00:01:25,475 --> 00:01:26,351
though other scientists

63
00:01:26,351 --> 00:01:27,965
dispute that.

64
00:01:27,985 --> 00:01:28,961
Their ocean neighbors have

65
00:01:28,961 --> 00:01:29,706
tricks too.

66
00:01:29,726 --> 00:01:30,939
Fur seals sleep with both

67
00:01:30,939 --> 00:01:32,476
halves at once on land,

68
00:01:32,496 --> 00:01:33,375
but in the water they

69
00:01:33,375 --> 00:01:34,461
mostly sleep one half at

70
00:01:34,461 --> 00:01:35,095
a time.

71
00:01:35,115 --> 00:01:36,545
And sperm whales take short

72
00:01:36,545 --> 00:01:37,889
naps hanging upright near the

73
00:01:37,889 --> 00:01:39,407
surface, like giant floating

74
00:01:39,407 --> 00:01:41,108
logs, for about seven percent

75
00:01:41,108 --> 00:01:41,660
of their time.

76
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:44,077
How good is this trick?

77
00:01:44,097 --> 00:01:45,017
In one study,

78
00:01:45,037 --> 00:01:46,448
two Navy dolphins were trained

79
00:01:46,448 --> 00:01:47,819
to keep finding targets with

80
00:01:47,819 --> 00:01:50,064
echolocation, their natural sonar.

81
00:01:50,084 --> 00:01:50,984
One of them kept it

82
00:01:50,984 --> 00:01:52,364
up for 15 days straight,

83
00:01:52,384 --> 00:01:53,713
without slipping.

84
00:01:53,733 --> 00:01:54,941
Bix lasts about fifteen

85
00:01:54,941 --> 00:01:56,973
minutes in a meeting.

86
00:01:56,993 --> 00:01:58,610
Dolphins aren't the only ones.

87
00:01:58,630 --> 00:01:59,584
Ducks sleeping at the edge

88
00:01:59,584 --> 00:02:00,537
of a group keep more

89
00:02:00,537 --> 00:02:01,420
of their brain awake,

90
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:02,537
with the open eye pointed

91
00:02:02,537 --> 00:02:04,068
outward, toward danger.

92
00:02:04,088 --> 00:02:05,123
And great frigatebirds can

93
00:02:05,123 --> 00:02:06,142
sleep while flying over the

94
00:02:06,142 --> 00:02:07,769
ocean, on trips lasting up

95
00:02:07,769 --> 00:02:08,738
to ten days.

96
00:02:08,758 --> 00:02:09,509
In one study,

97
00:02:09,509 --> 00:02:10,949
they slept only about 40

98
00:02:10,949 --> 00:02:12,025
minutes a day in the

99
00:02:12,025 --> 00:02:13,498
air, compared with over 12

100
00:02:13,498 --> 00:02:14,515
hours a day on land.

101
00:02:15,865 --> 00:02:17,123
Now, what about you?

102
00:02:17,143 --> 00:02:18,869
Surprise: you already do a

103
00:02:18,869 --> 00:02:19,653
tiny version.

104
00:02:19,673 --> 00:02:20,860
The first night you sleep

105
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:21,513
somewhere new,

106
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:22,293
like a hotel,

107
00:02:22,313 --> 00:02:23,576
part of your left hemisphere

108
00:02:23,576 --> 00:02:24,952
stays more alert during deep

109
00:02:24,952 --> 00:02:26,712
sleep, and reacts faster to

110
00:02:26,712 --> 00:02:27,800
strange sounds.

111
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:28,843
Scientists call it the

112
00:02:28,843 --> 00:02:29,730
first-night effect,

113
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:31,052
a kind of built-in night

114
00:02:31,052 --> 00:02:32,719
watch for unfamiliar places.

115
00:02:32,739 --> 00:02:33,809
It's one reason your first

116
00:02:33,809 --> 00:02:34,809
night away from home is

117
00:02:34,809 --> 00:02:36,473
often your worst.

118
00:02:36,493 --> 00:02:37,393
But what if you had

119
00:02:37,393 --> 00:02:38,389
the full upgrade?

120
00:02:38,409 --> 00:02:39,270
One half sleeps,

121
00:02:39,270 --> 00:02:40,466
the other half handles your

122
00:02:40,466 --> 00:02:42,156
day, then they switch.

123
00:02:42,176 --> 00:02:44,103
No more yawning through first period.

124
00:02:44,123 --> 00:02:45,581
Sounds perfect.

125
00:02:45,601 --> 00:02:47,558
There's just one catch.

126
00:02:47,578 --> 00:02:48,551
Actually, three.

127
00:02:49,901 --> 00:02:50,631
Catch one:

128
00:02:50,651 --> 00:02:51,710
your two halves don't do

129
00:02:51,710 --> 00:02:52,630
the same jobs.

130
00:02:52,650 --> 00:02:53,980
In most right-handed people,

131
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,761
language lives mainly in the

132
00:02:55,761 --> 00:02:56,951
left hemisphere.

133
00:02:56,971 --> 00:02:57,965
So with your left half

134
00:02:57,965 --> 00:02:59,761
asleep, you'd be awake,

135
00:02:59,781 --> 00:03:01,481
but you might struggle to talk.

136
00:03:01,501 --> 00:03:03,687
Or to read your homework.

137
00:03:03,707 --> 00:03:04,557
Catch two:

138
00:03:04,577 --> 00:03:05,640
each half of your brain

139
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:07,011
controls the opposite side of

140
00:03:07,011 --> 00:03:07,733
your body.

141
00:03:07,753 --> 00:03:09,026
Left half asleep?

142
00:03:09,046 --> 00:03:10,513
Your right hand gets clumsy.

143
00:03:10,533 --> 00:03:12,231
Hope you're not right-handed.

144
00:03:12,251 --> 00:03:12,951
Oh wait,

145
00:03:12,971 --> 00:03:13,483
most people are.

146
00:03:14,483 --> 00:03:15,513
Catch three:

147
00:03:15,533 --> 00:03:16,362
your eyes are wired

148
00:03:16,362 --> 00:03:17,826
differently from a dolphin's.

149
00:03:17,846 --> 00:03:19,031
About half the nerve fibers

150
00:03:19,031 --> 00:03:19,991
from each of your eyes

151
00:03:19,991 --> 00:03:21,386
cross to the other side,

152
00:03:21,406 --> 00:03:22,432
so each eye talks to

153
00:03:22,432 --> 00:03:23,713
both halves of your brain.

154
00:03:23,733 --> 00:03:25,080
One sleepy half couldn't just

155
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:25,953
switch off one eye.

156
00:03:26,953 --> 00:03:28,403
And the biggest catch:

157
00:03:28,423 --> 00:03:29,778
it wouldn't give you free time.

158
00:03:29,798 --> 00:03:31,445
Dolphins still sleep.

159
00:03:31,465 --> 00:03:32,645
Each half still gets its

160
00:03:32,645 --> 00:03:34,145
rest, just in shifts.

161
00:03:34,165 --> 00:03:35,414
Your brain would still need

162
00:03:35,414 --> 00:03:36,350
its hours too.

163
00:03:36,370 --> 00:03:36,860
For adults,

164
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:37,889
that's at least seven hours

165
00:03:37,889 --> 00:03:38,501
a night.

166
00:03:38,521 --> 00:03:39,046
For teens,

167
00:03:39,046 --> 00:03:39,833
it's eight to ten.

168
00:03:40,833 --> 00:03:42,206
So Bix tried it.

169
00:03:42,226 --> 00:03:42,941
One eye open,

170
00:03:42,941 --> 00:03:44,442
half the brain on duty...

171
00:03:44,462 --> 00:03:45,717
and both halves fell asleep

172
00:03:45,717 --> 00:03:47,092
in about four seconds.

173
00:03:47,112 --> 00:03:48,170
Newton kept watch.

174
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:51,204
Next time on What If

175
00:03:51,204 --> 00:03:52,673
Bix: what if dads had

176
00:03:52,673 --> 00:03:53,660
to sit on the egg,

177
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:54,480
all winter long?

178
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:56,992
Thanks for watching with both

179
00:03:56,992 --> 00:03:57,976
halves of your brain.

180
00:03:57,996 --> 00:03:59,234
Or at least one of them.

181
00:03:59,254 --> 00:04:00,202
Watch another what if right

182
00:04:00,202 --> 00:04:01,806
here, and subscribe before you

183
00:04:01,806 --> 00:04:02,437
fall asleep.
